      "id": "downtime",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 200
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "start",
        "title": "Downtime",
        "description": "Downtime between scenes, standing in for the map. Keep it short and alive: answer what the player does or asks with the town as it is (people, prices, gossip, the Assembly preparations), in the house style, from the lore where it says. If they ask around, people know the everyday things the lore gives them and pass on gossip. The lead points onward: The big man with the bandaged hand, whom Kell saw at a gentleman's elbow by the Purveyor's wagons at noon, went down the river stairs into the Drowned Rat; she followed him there, and means to go back down at dusk (dock gossip says the big men who do night work for gentlemen drink there). If they haven't got it, let it reach them through whatever they're doing (a porter's gossip, a shopkeeper, or Kell saying where she's off to on her own errand). Don't start new adventures here: places that aren't on the way are for another day.\n\nAs it opens: a little time has passed since the last scene ended (the hand-off says how): the player has left that place and is out in the town now (the market square, an hour or two after the granary). In a line or two, say where the others from the last scene went: Sergeant Kell stays with the investigator (the King lent her to the case) unless what happened rules it out; anyone else goes their own way. Then show the town as it is now: the Assembly eve (bunting, sweepers, delegation banners, dear bread, the Corn Exchange's price board) and the city too busy to care about a hanged clerk. From Caerwyn now, show what's new since the last scene: one or two things, by sight or by talk, each said once by someone it matters to. Two or three vivid details in all, no more. End with the player on their own feet, and ask what they do.",
        "cast": {
          "kell": "You're on your own errand (your card says what it is at this hour): say where you'll be and when, once, then go about it, with the investigator or without. Your own dry view of the town and the case, when it's wanted."
        },
        "lore": []
      }
    },
